Here’s how you can wire everything together:

```js
// 1️⃣ Create the queue – note that all arguments are passed as **stringified** values
const queue = functions.B({
  e: "[]",          // start with an empty array of tasks
  t: "5",           // concurrency level of 5
  n: "0"            // optional payload size (default is 0)
});

// 2️⃣ Attach the saturation callbacks
queue.on("saturated", () => console.log("Queue is saturated"));
queue.on("unsaturated", () => console.log("Queue is unsaturated"));

// 3️⃣ Add some demo tasks
for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  queue.add(() => console.log(`Executing task #${i + 1}`));
}
```

### What this does

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| 1️⃣ | Calls `functions.B` with a stringified empty array (`"[]"`) as the initial task list, sets concurrency to `"5"`, and leaves payload at its default (`"0"`). The function returns a queue instance. |
| 2️⃣ | Registers two event listeners on the returned queue: one fires when the queue reaches its maximum concurrent workers (“saturated”), and the other fires when it drops below that threshold (“unsaturated”). |
| 3️⃣ | Adds ten simple logging tasks to the queue. Because the concurrency is 5, five tasks will run immediately; once any finishes, the next queued task starts, keeping the queue saturated until fewer than five tasks remain running. |

Feel free to replace the demo tasks with your real work‑doing functions—just make sure they’re functions that return a promise if you want async behavior.
